Q: How to set CultureInfo.CurrentCulture? A: <p>You could just define a key in your App.config like this</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;configuration&gt;
    &lt;appSettings&gt;
        &lt;add key=&quot;DefaultCulture&quot; value=&quot;en-CA&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/appSettings&gt;
&lt;/configuration&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>and in your application read that value and set the culture</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>CultureInfo culture = new CultureInfo(ConfigurationManager.AppSettings[&quot;DefaultCulture&quot;]);
Thread.CurrentThread.CurrentCulture = culture;
Thread.CurrentThread.CurrentUICulture = culture;
</code></pre>

Q: How to implement generic deserialization methods with Kotlinx.serialization without changing the abstract class? A: <p>As what tyg mentioned, you can't use <code>reified</code> generics for abstract methods in an abstract class.</p>
<p>The good news is that kotlinx.serialization still allows you to pass the <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/api/kotlinx.serialization/kotlinx-serialization-core/kotlinx.serialization/-k-serializer/" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>KSerializer</code></a> implementation of the relevant class you're trying to serialise/de-serialise, which can be obtained from the <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/api/kotlinx.serialization/kotlinx-serialization-core/kotlinx.serialization/serializer.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">top-level <code>serializer</code> method</a>.</p>
<p>Note that you'll have to either pass a <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/api/core/kotlin-stdlib/kotlin.reflect/-k-type/" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>KType</code></a> (see <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/42862966/how-to-obtain-a-ktype-in-kotlin">How to obtain a KType in Kotlin?</a> on how to obtain it) or a <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/api/core/kotlin-stdlib/kotlin.reflect/-k-class/" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>KClass</code></a> (which is experimental):</p>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>// This is the API signature from kotlinx.serialization
fun serializer(type: KType): KSerializer { ... }

// And this is how you would use it
class Example
val javaClass = Example::class.java
val example = serializer(javaClass.kotlin.createType())
</code></pre>
<p>With that, you can modify your concrete class implementation accordingly:</p>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>import kotlinx.serialization.json.Json as KotlinXJson // Tip: You can use import aliases so that you don't have to keep using fully-qualified names
import kotlinx.serialization.serializer

class KotlinDependencyProvider() : JsonDependencyProvider {

    @OptIn(ExperimentalSerializationApi::class)
    override val json: Eval&lt;Json&gt; = Eval.later {
        object : Json() {

            override fun &lt;A&gt; fromJson(ins: InputStream, type: Class&lt;A&gt;): A {
                val serializer = serializer(type.kotlin.createType()) // Obtain the KSerializer of your type you're passing in

                return InputStreamReader(ins).use {
                    val value =  KotlinXJson.decodeFromStream&lt;A&gt;(deserializer = serializer, stream = it)
                    value
                }
            }

            override fun &lt;A&gt; fromJson(json: String, type: Class&lt;A&gt;): Either&lt;Throwable, A&gt; {
                val serializer = serializer(type.kotlin.createType())
                return kotlin.runCatching {
                    val value = KotlinXJson.decodeFromString(deserializer = , string = json)
                    value?.right() ?: Exception(&quot;Empty json&quot;).left()
                }.fold({
                    it
                }, {
                    it.left()
                })
            }

            override fun toJson(obj: Any): String {
                return KotlinXJson.encodeToString(obj)
            }

            override fun toJson(obj: Any, outputStream: OutputStream) {
                OutputStreamWriter(outputStream).use {
                    KotlinXJson.encodeToStream(obj, outputStream)
                    it.flush()
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>(If you're wondering, the <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/api/kotlinx.serialization/kotlinx-serialization-core/kotlinx.serialization/-k-serializer/" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>KSerializer</code></a> interface implements both <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/api/kotlinx.serialization/kotlinx-serialization-core/kotlinx.serialization/-deserialization-strategy/index.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>DeserializationStrategy</code></a> and <a href="https://kotlinlang.org/api/kotlinx.serialization/kotlinx-serialization-core/kotlinx.serialization/-serialization-strategy/index.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>SerializationStrategy</code></a> interfaces, which does only de-serialising and serialising respectively)</p>
<hr />
<p>As for using a <code>Writer</code>, I don't think that's possible unless you convert it to an <code>OutputStream</code> (for example, using <a href="https://commons.apache.org/proper/commons-io/apidocs/org/apache/commons/io/output/WriterOutputStream.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Apache Commons' <code>WriterOutputStream</code></a> - see <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/62241/how-to-convert-a-reader-to-inputstream-and-a-writer-to-outputstream">How to convert a Reader to InputStream and a Writer to OutputStream?</a> for more info).</p>
<p>Alternatively, you may take a look at the <a href="https://github.com/Kotlin/kotlinx-io/issues/464" rel="nofollow noreferrer">feature request for the kotlinx-io variant of kotlinx.serialization to add <code>Writer</code> support</a>.</p>

Q: std::make_signed that accepts floating point types A: <p>A simple template alias will do:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;type_traits&gt;

template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct identity { using type = T; };

template&lt;typename T&gt;
using try_make_signed =
    typename std::conditional&lt;
        std::is_integral&lt;T&gt;::value,
        std::make_signed&lt;T&gt;,
        identity&lt;T&gt;
        &gt;::type;
</code></pre>
<p>And this is how you could test it:</p>
<pre><code>int main()
{
    static_assert(std::is_same&lt;
        try_make_signed&lt;unsigned int&gt;::type, int
        &gt;::value, &quot;!&quot;);

    static_assert(std::is_same&lt;
        try_make_signed&lt;double&gt;::type, double
        &gt;::value, &quot;!&quot;);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here is a <a href="http://ideone.com/pF1stw" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><strong>live example</strong></a>.</p>
